  <header class="qa-header">
    <p class="qa-pillar">Species Sub-page · SPC·12r</p>
    <h1>Somali Crow Range: Where This Species Lives</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Somali Crow (<em>Corvus edithae</em>), also known as the Dwarf Raven, is confined to the Horn of Africa — primarily Somalia, with records from adjacent Djibouti and eastern Ethiopia. It is one of the most restricted-range corvids in the world and one of the least-studied birds globally, due to the prolonged inaccessibility of Somalia to ornithological research.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Known Distribution</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Somali Crow's distribution is based on limited records:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Somalia:</strong> the core of the range. Records are scattered from northern Somalia (Somaliland), central Somalia, and southern Somalia, but density and exact distribution within the country are essentially unknown due to the absence of systematic surveys since the 1980s.</li>
      <li><strong>Djibouti:</strong> a few records from the arid interior, representing the northwestern edge of the range.</li>
      <li><strong>Ethiopia (Ogaden/Somali Region):</strong> the Ogaden arid zone — the adjacent area of eastern Ethiopia with similar habitat to Somalia — has records, though these are also sparse.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The limits of the range remain uncertain. The species may extend further into Ethiopia and Eritrea than current records suggest, or its range may be genuinely very small. Without surveys, it is impossible to determine range accurately.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Habitat</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Based on the habitat type where most records come from:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Arid thorn-scrub (Acacia-Commiphora woodland and bush), dry acacia scrubland, and open semi-desert terrain.</li>
      <li>Avoids dense forest and highland habitats — these are occupied by other corvids (the Thick-billed Raven in the Ethiopian Highlands, the Brown-necked Raven in adjacent arid zones).</li>
      <li>Likely forages in open terrain similar to the Brown-necked Raven — ground foraging for invertebrates, small vertebrates, and scavenging.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Research Gaps</h2>
    <p>The Somali Crow is among the corvids most urgently in need of basic range surveys. The situation in Somalia has precluded ornithological fieldwork for over three decades. Future surveys in Somaliland (the more accessible self-declared region of northern Somalia), adjacent Djibouti, and the Ethiopian Ogaden could substantially improve knowledge of this species' range, population, and ecology.</p>
  </section>

